---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import { site } from '../../content';
const path = '/for/print-on-demand';
const title = 'Shopify SMS apps for print-on-demand stores | ' + site.name;
const desc = 'Evaluate SMS apps for print-on-demand with blank availability, artwork approval, production queues, delivery estimates, and reprint costs.';
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden py-16 md:py-24">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/for" class="link text-sm">← All store types</a>
      <p class="badge mt-6">Print-on-demand stores</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">Choose Shopify SMS apps around production readiness</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-orange-950/80">A design can be available while its blank, print method, or destination is unavailable. Choose a messaging tool by testing whether your team can keep promotions aligned with what the production partner can actually make and deliver.</p>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-3xl text-lg leading-8 text-orange-950/75">Use SMS for a specific, relevant buying decision. Put artwork instructions, size charts, and detailed production explanations on a linked page or in email. The fixtures below are fictional proposed acceptance tests, not merchant results or promises about app behavior.</p>
      <div class="mt-9 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/apps">Browse app profiles</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/guides/campaign-restraint">Campaign restraint</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify card">
      <h2>A shortlist based on the work you need to own</h2>
      <p><a class="link" href="https://postscript.io/features">Postscript describes SMS marketing built for Shopify</a>. It is a candidate when a merchant wants to evaluate a dedicated SMS program around design launches and requested availability alerts. In a demo, ask the operator to suppress an unavailable blank variant after an audience has already been queued. Require evidence of how the production partner's data reaches the sender; Shopify order data alone does not prove that a printer has capacity.</p>
      <p><a class="link" href="https://www.omnisend.com/shopify-email-marketing/">Omnisend offers email and SMS marketing for Shopify</a>. It is a candidate when one team wants to evaluate both channels for a design launch. Test whether an artwork-help email and a promotional text can be coordinated without sending the text to a buyer whose order is waiting for a proof. Confirm current destination support, plan inclusions, and event handling with the vendor.</p>
      <p>These are conditional fits, not a ranking. Neither channel description establishes a native integration with your print provider, an artwork approval workflow, or a delivery guarantee. Compare the current quote and a working event demonstration before choosing. Existing email tooling may be sufficient if your team can reliably share consent, purchase exits, and production exceptions.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Proposed acceptance fixtures for a made-to-order catalog</h2>
      <p>All stores and events in this section are fictional. Run these cases with test profiles and preview messages before enabling customer sends. Passing a fixture is an acceptance criterion for your implementation, not a claim that a listed app passes automatically.</p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto">
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Fixture</th><th>Input to test</th><th>Required behavior</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Studio Finch: design launch</td><td>The design is active, but the requested navy blank in one size is unavailable at the selected printer.</td><td>Exclude that unavailable combination from the promotion. Verify the destination page shows only fulfillable options; do not call made-to-order stock a limited edition without a real edition limit.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Petal Press: personalized gift</td><td>A buyer has paid but has not approved the spelling on a custom print.</td><td>Exit cart recovery, route the proof issue to the responsible team, and block unrelated urgency messages while it is unresolved. A proof reminder must link to the correct order and approval state.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Mapline Goods: event merchandise</td><td>The printer revises its production estimate after a campaign has been prepared.</td><td>Pause the queued campaign, revise the landing page and copy, and review destination-specific transit assumptions. An estimated dispatch date must not become a guaranteed arrival date.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>North Window: reprint request</td><td>A customer reports an off-center print and support opens a replacement case.</td><td>Suppress promotional winback while the case is open. A replacement event must not create a new marketing opt-in or count as a second paid purchase.</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Ink Trail: delayed partner feed</td><td>An availability event is duplicated, arrives late, or lacks a variant identifier.</td><td>Deduplicate valid events and stop the affected send when availability cannot be verified. Record the exception for an operator; do not fill missing fields with optimistic defaults.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Define the production contract before the campaign</h2>
      <p>Keep a mapping from Shopify variant to provider product, blank color and size, print method, destination, and the latest verified availability. Assign an owner to investigate stale or unmapped records. Separate payment, artwork approval, production acceptance, dispatch, and delivery states so a purchase does not imply that every later step has happened.</p>
      <p>Choose an event freshness limit based on your provider's update behavior and your tolerance for overselling. If the limit is exceeded, pause affected promotions until the operator verifies the record. Recheck the eligible audience at send time, including orders placed after scheduling, open complaints, opt-outs, and held-out profiles. Test replayed events and purchases made in another browser.</p>
      <p>For a launch, start with customers who requested that design or a relevant style and have appropriate channel permission. Set contact limits based on the capture promise and observed response. A new design is not permission to send every subscriber an alert. An availability alert for a blank should identify the relevant size or color rather than imply that every variant is ready.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Evaluate contribution after production exceptions</h2>
      <p>Compare net sales per eligible contact in the messaged group with a comparable randomized holdout over the same observation window. Include enough time for production problems and refunds to appear. Platform-attributed sales are not a measurement of incremental lift; small audiences and overlapping campaigns can leave the result uncertain.</p>
      <p>Net sales should already reflect discounts and refunds. Subtract blank and printing costs, fulfillment and merchant-funded shipping, payment fees, reprint costs, support labor, message and carrier charges, and the relevant platform cost allocation. Do not subtract discounts or refunds again if they are already included in net sales. Count replacement costs without treating free replacements as new revenue.</p>
      <p>Compare contribution per eligible contact across groups with costs allocated consistently. Include fixed platform costs in the stack decision even if they do not change during the test. Ask for a quote covering your destination mix, message encoding and billable segments, peak sends, contact growth, and any middleware needed for provider events. Use <a class="link" href="/guides/pricing">the pricing guide</a> as a worksheet prompt rather than relying on a headline message price.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify card mt-8">
      <h2>Release controls and stop conditions</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Retain channel-specific permission evidence and capture wording; a custom artwork submission or order phone number alone does not establish promotional permission.</li>
        <li>Validate destination rules and recipient-time sending restrictions for the markets you serve. Use <a class="link" href="/guides/consent">the consent guide</a> to prepare questions for the responsible reviewer.</li>
        <li>Test opt-out processing, reply routing, and suppression synchronization across the tools in the actual configuration.</li>
        <li>Verify every personalization link against the intended test order and restrict access to customer artwork and proof details.</li>
        <li>Assign a person who can stop sends when blank availability, production estimates, suppression sync, or landing-page accuracy fails.</li>
        <li>Define complaint and cancellation review thresholds before release; resolve the cause before increasing reach.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Approve the app only after the fixtures pass with your production partner and current plan. Preserve consent and suppression records during any migration, then rerun the same fixtures. For a broader channel comparison, see <a class="link" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-omnisend">Klaviyo vs Omnisend</a> and evaluate the actual event path your store needs.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
